"""Independent teaching sequence, using the mathematical order of sections 12.1–12.4."""
import sympy as s
t=s.Symbol('t');lessons=[];models=[]
def T(x):return s.latex(s.sympify(x))
def D(*rows):return r'\[\begin{gathered}'+r'\\'.join(rows)+r'\end{gathered}\]'
def stats(g):
 m=s.simplify(s.diff(g,t).subs(t,1));f=s.simplify(s.diff(g,t,2).subs(t,1));return m,f,s.simplify(f+m-m*m)
def example(uid,title,question,solution,g,extra=None):
 models.append(dict(id=uid,pgfs={k:str(v) for k,v in g.items()},**(extra or {})))
 return dict(title=title,question=question,solution=solution,verification=uid)
def lesson(key,title,summary,objective,before,ideas,method,mistakes,examples):lessons.append(dict(key=key,id='stats_pgf_'+key,title=title,summary=summary,objective=objective,before=before,ideas=ideas,method=method,mistakes=mistakes,examples=examples))
base='/courses/maths4u-9231-statistics/topics/stats_pgf_'
g=(2+t**2+2*t**4)/5
e1=example('PGF-E-B1','1 · Read the powers as possible counts',
 r'<p>A batch count \(X\) takes values \(0,2,4\) with probabilities \(\frac25,\frac15,\frac25\). Build its PGF and find \(\mathrm P(X=3)\).</p>',
 D(r'G_X(t)=\frac25+\frac15t^2+\frac25t^4',r'G_X(1)=1,\qquad [t^3]G_X(t)=0')+'<p>Each probability multiplies a power whose exponent is the count. There is no degree-3 term, so that value is impossible. A missing term means zero probability.</p>',{'X':g},dict(weights=[['0','2/5'],['2','1/5'],['4','2/5']],checks=[dict(expression='0',value='0')]))
p=s.Rational(2,7);g=p*t/(1-(1-p)*t)
e2=example('PGF-E-B2','2 · Count attempts or count failures',
 r'<p>Independent trials succeed with probability \(\frac27\). Let \(T\) include the first success, and let \(F\) count only the preceding failures. Derive both PGFs and find \(\mathrm P(T>4)\).</p>',
 D(r'\mathrm P(T=r)=\frac27\left(\frac57\right)^{r-1},\quad r\ge1',r'G_T(t)=\frac{2t}{7}\sum_{j=0}^\infty\left(\frac{5t}{7}\right)^j=\frac{2t}{7-5t}',r'G_F(t)=\frac{2}{7-5t}',r'\mathrm P(T>4)=\left(\frac57\right)^4=\frac{625}{2401}')+r'<p>The two supports start at 1 and 0 respectively. Both series converge for \(|t|&lt;\frac75\). More than four attempts requires the first four trials to fail.</p>',{'T':g,'F':g/t},dict(geometric='2/7',checks=[dict(expression='(5/7)**4',value='625/2401')]))
g=sum(t**j for j in range(4))/4
e3=example('PGF-E-B3','3 · A finite uniform count',
 r'<p>A random setting \(X\) is uniform on \(\{0,1,2,3\}\). Find its PGF as a polynomial and as a fraction, and explain the value at \(t=1\).</p>',
 D(r'G_X(t)=\frac{1+t+t^2+t^3}{4}',r'G_X(t)=\frac{1-t^4}{4(1-t)},\quad t\ne1',r'G_X(1)=1')+'<p>The fraction is a finite geometric-sum identity. Its apparent singularity at 1 is removable: the original polynomial is defined there, and its coefficients sum to 1.</p>',{'X':g},dict(uniform=[0,3]))
lam=s.Rational(4,3);g=s.exp(lam*(t-1))
e4=example('PGF-E-B4','4 · A Poisson count and its parity',
 r'<p>\(X\) is Poisson with mean \(\frac43\). Derive its PGF from the mass function and find the probability that \(X\) is even, including zero.</p>',
 D(r'G_X(t)=e^{-4/3}\sum_{r=0}^{\infty}\frac{(4t/3)^r}{r!}=e^{\frac43(t-1)}',r'G_X(-1)=\mathrm P(\text{even})-\mathrm P(\text{odd})=e^{-8/3}',r'\mathrm P(\text{even})=\frac{1+e^{-8/3}}2')+'<p>The two parity probabilities also sum to 1. Evaluating at −1 changes the signs of the odd-power terms; it does not discard them.</p>',{'X':g},dict(poisson='4/3',checks=[dict(expression='(1+exp(-8/3))/2',value='(1+exp(-8/3))/2')]))
lesson('construct','Building and reading generating functions','Encode a discrete distribution in the coefficients and powers of one function.',
 'Construct a valid PGF and recover probabilities, support and named count models.',
 '<p>Review discrete probability, binomial and Poisson models, and geometric series. Unlike a continuous density, a discrete mass function assigns probabilities to individual values.</p>',
 r'''<h3>A distribution written as a power series</h3><p>For a non-negative integer-valued X, attach probability P(X=r) to the power tʳ. The variable t is an algebraic input; it is not the outcome.</p>'''+D(r'G_X(t)=\mathrm E(t^X)=\sum_{r=0}^{\infty}p_rt^r',r'p_r\ge0,\qquad \sum_{r=0}^{\infty}p_r=1',r'G_X(1)=1,\qquad G_X(0)=\mathrm P(X=0)')+r'''<p>Every missing power has coefficient zero. Normalisation alone is insufficient: a negative coefficient cannot represent a probability. For finite support the series is a polynomial. An infinite probability series converges at least for |t|≤1; a closed rational expression must be interpreted with its series and convergence range.</p><h3>Recognise common constructions</h3>'''+D(r'X\sim\operatorname{Bin}(n,p):\quad G_X(t)=(1-p+pt)^n',r'X\sim\operatorname{Po}(\lambda):\quad G_X(t)=e^{\lambda(t-1)}',r'T=\text{trials including first success}:\quad G_T(t)=\frac{pt}{1-(1-p)t}',r'F=\text{failures before success}:\quad G_F(t)=\frac{p}{1-(1-p)t}')+r'''<p>The geometric model needs independent trials with a constant success probability, 0&lt;p&lt;1. Its two counting conventions differ by one. For a capped process, the final value includes every path that reaches the cap, even paths with no success.</p><h3>Coefficient extraction and integer tails</h3>'''+D(r'\mathrm P(X=r)=[t^r]G_X(t)=\frac{G_X^{(r)}(0)}{r!}')+r'''<p>Use derivatives at 0 for coefficients, not at 1. For geometric tails, translate a strict event such as X&lt;N into the actual integer values before using a complement. After a logarithmic inequality, check both neighbouring integers. Division by a negative logarithm reverses the inequality.</p>''',
 '<ol><li>State the support and the mass at each value.</li><li>Form the defining expectation or power series.</li><li>Sum or factorise the expression, with its appropriate domain.</li><li>Check non-negative coefficients and G(1)=1.</li><li>Read the requested coefficient or sum the correct integer tail.</li></ol>',
 ['Exponents are values of the count, not probabilities.','G(1)=1 does not excuse a negative coefficient.','Trials including success and failures before success have different supports.','Keep strict and inclusive integer tails distinct.','The coefficient formula uses derivatives at 0 and divides by r factorial.'],[e1,e2,e3,e4])

g=(3+2*t+t**3)/6;m,f,v=stats(g)
e1=example('PGF-E-M1','1 · The second derivative is a factorial moment',r'<p>For \(G_X(t)=\frac{3+2t+t^3}{6}\), find \(\mathrm E(X)\), \(\mathrm E(X^2)\) and \(\operatorname{Var}(X)\).</p>',
 D(r"G'_X(1)=\frac56,\qquad G''_X(1)=1",r'\mathrm E(X^2)=1+\frac56=\frac{11}{6}',r'\operatorname{Var}(X)=\frac{11}{6}-\left(\frac56\right)^2=\frac{41}{36}')+'<p>The second derivative supplies E[X(X−1)]. Add the first moment before subtracting the square of the mean.</p>',{'X':g},dict(moments={'X':[str(m),str(f),str(v)]}))
g=(1+3*t+2*t**2)/6;m,f,v=stats(g)
e2=example('PGF-E-M2','2 · Reconstruct all probabilities',r'<p>\(X\) takes values \(0,1,2\). Its mean is \(\frac76\) and variance is \(\frac{17}{36}\). Recover its whole PGF.</p>',
 D(r'a+b+c=1',r'b+2c=\frac76',r'b+4c=\frac{17}{36}+\left(\frac76\right)^2=\frac{11}{6}',r'c=\frac13,\quad b=\frac12,\quad a=\frac16',r'G_X(t)=\frac{1+3t+2t^2}{6}')+'<p>Subtract the two moment equations to find c, then solve for the other coefficients. All three are non-negative and sum to 1; these checks are part of reconstructing a distribution.</p>',{'X':g},dict(moments={'X':[str(m),str(f),str(v)]},recover=['7/6','17/36']))
g=t**2/(5-4*t);m,f,v=stats(g)
e3=example('PGF-E-M3','3 · A shifted infinite-support model',r'<p>A count has \(G_X(t)=\frac{t^2}{5-4t}\). Use its PGF to find the mean and variance.</p>',
 D(r'G_X(t)=t^2\frac{1/5}{1-(4/5)t}',r"G'_X(1)=6,\qquad G''_X(1)=50",r'\operatorname{Var}(X)=50+6-6^2=20')+'<p>The factor t² shifts a geometric failure count by 2. The mean shifts, while the variance is unchanged by that constant. Direct differentiation agrees with this interpretation.</p>',{'X':g},dict(moments={'X':[str(m),str(f),str(v)]}))
g=(s.Rational(3,4)+t/4)**12
e4=example('PGF-E-M4','4 · Recover a binomial model from two moments',r'<p>\(X\) is binomial with unknown \(n,p\). Its mean is \(3\) and variance is \(\frac94\). Recover \(n,p\) and the PGF.</p>',
 D(r'G_X(t)=(1-p+pt)^n',r"G'_X(1)=np,\qquad G''_X(1)=n(n-1)p^2",r'np=3,\qquad np(1-p)=\frac94',r'p=\frac14,\qquad n=12',r'G_X(t)=\left(\frac34+\frac14t\right)^{12}')+'<p>The ratio of variance to mean is 1−p. The resulting n must be a positive integer and p must lie in [0,1].</p>',{'X':g},dict(binomial=[12,'1/4']))
lesson('moments','Recovering distributions and moments','Use derivatives at one, and keep ordinary and factorial moments separate.',
 'Find means and variances from PGFs and recover unknown probability parameters.',
 '<p>Complete <a href="'+base+'construct">building and reading PGFs</a>. Review differentiation, linear equations and Var(X)=E(X²)−[E(X)]².</p>',
 r'''<h3>Why the evaluation point changes</h3><p>Differentiating each term multiplies its coefficient by the exponent. Evaluation at 1 then sums the weighted probabilities. Where these moments are finite,</p>'''+D(r"G'_X(1)=\sum_r rp_r=\mathrm E(X)",r"G''_X(1)=\sum_r r(r-1)p_r=\mathrm E[X(X-1)]",r"\mathrm E(X^2)=G''_X(1)+G'_X(1)",r"\operatorname{Var}(X)=G''_X(1)+G'_X(1)-[G'_X(1)]^2")+r'''<p>For an infinite distribution, use the derivative at 1 or its left limit when the defining series requires it. An infinite moment is not made finite by formal substitution. All models used in the written questions here have the moments required.</p><h3>Recover coefficients by independent conditions</h3><p>Start with normalisation. A known mean gives a first weighted equation; a known variance gives a second-moment equation after adding the square of the mean. Solve all equations together and check non-negativity. A negative algebraic solution is not a valid probability.</p><h3>Parameters have restrictions</h3><p>A binomial trial count is a non-negative integer and its success probability is between 0 and 1. A Poisson parameter is positive. For repeated-copy reconstruction, the candidate root must be a probability series, not merely an algebraic root. The original model restrictions decide which solutions remain.</p>''',
 '<ol><li>Normalise any unknown coefficients.</li><li>Differentiate before substituting t=1.</li><li>Record the first and second factorial moments.</li><li>Convert to E(X²) and variance.</li><li>Solve remaining parameters and check all probability and model restrictions.</li></ol>',
 ['G″(1) is not E(X²).','Subtract the square of the mean, not the mean itself.','Use derivatives at 1 for moments, at 0 for coefficients.','Check every recovered coefficient is non-negative.','A formal root or non-integral binomial trial count may be inadmissible.'],[e1,e2,e3,e4])

x=(3+2*t)/5;y=(2+t**2)/3;z=s.expand(x*y)
e1=example('PGF-E-A1','1 · Convolution appears as multiplication',r'<p>Independent counts have \(G_X(t)=\frac{3+2t}{5}\) and \(G_Y(t)=\frac{2+t^2}{3}\). Find the PGF of \(Z=X+Y\) and \(\mathrm P(Z=2)\).</p>',
 D(r'G_Z(t)=G_X(t)G_Y(t)',r'=\frac{6+4t+3t^2+2t^3}{15}',r'\mathrm P(Z=2)=\frac15')+'<p>The degree-2 coefficient comes from X=0 and Y=2. Coefficients of other degrees collect every pair with that total. Independence allows each joint probability to factor.</p>',{'X':x,'Y':y,'Z':z},dict(independentSum=['X','Y','Z']))
x=(6+12*t+3*t**2)/21;y=(3+2*t)/5;z=s.expand(x*y)
e2=example('PGF-E-A2','2 · Dependence inside one selection is allowed',
 r'<p>Choose two counters without replacement from three marked and four unmarked counters; \(X\) counts marked counters. Independently generate a binary \(Y\) with \(\mathrm P(Y=1)=\frac25\). Find \(G_{X+Y}(t)\).</p>',
 D(r'\mathrm P(X=r)=\frac{\binom3r\binom4{2-r}}{\binom72}',r'G_X(t)=\frac{6+12t+3t^2}{21}',r'G_{X+Y}(t)=\frac{18+48t+33t^2+6t^3}{105}')+'<p>The two draws within the counter selection are dependent, so X is not binomial. The completed count X is independent of Y, which is exactly the independence needed for their sum.</p>',{'X':x,'Y':y,'Z':z},dict(hypergeometric=[3,4,2],independentSum=['X','Y','Z']))
x=s.exp(s.Rational(2,3)*(t-1));y=s.exp(s.Rational(5,6)*(t-1));z=x*y
e3=example('PGF-E-A3','3 · Add independent Poisson counts',r'<p>Independent counts \(X,Y\) are Poisson with means \(\frac23,\frac56\). Identify \(Z=X+Y\) using its PGF.</p>',
 D(r'G_Z(t)=e^{\frac23(t-1)}e^{\frac56(t-1)}',r'=e^{\frac32(t-1)}',r'Z\sim\operatorname{Po}(3/2)')+'<p>Multiplication adds the exponents. The resulting expression is a Poisson PGF with parameter equal to the sum of the two parameters. Independence is necessary for this argument.</p>',{'X':x,'Y':y,'Z':z},dict(independentSum=['X','Y','Z']))
x=2*t/(5-3*t);y=3*t/(4-t);z=x*y
e4=example('PGF-E-A4','4 · Two waiting times with different probabilities',r'<p>Independent waiting times include their first successes, with success probabilities \(\frac25,\frac34\). Find \(\mathrm P(X+Y=3)\), and the mean and variance of their total.</p>',
 D(r'G_Z(t)=\frac{6t^2}{(5-3t)(4-t)}',r'\mathrm P(Z=3)=\frac25\frac34\left(\frac35+\frac14\right)=\frac{51}{200}',r'\mathrm E(Z)=\frac52+\frac43=\frac{23}{6}',r'\operatorname{Var}(Z)=\frac{15}{4}+\frac49=\frac{151}{36}')+'<p>The coefficient has two contributions: waiting-time pairs (1,2) and (2,1). These probabilities differ, so counting the pairs without weighting them would be wrong.</p>',{'X':x,'Y':y,'Z':z},dict(independentSum=['X','Y','Z'],checks=[dict(expression='51/200',value='51/200')]))
lesson('sums','Adding independent counts','Use products only when the component counts are independent.',
 'Construct the PGF of an independent sum and extract its probabilities and moments.',
 '<p>Complete <a href="'+base+'moments">moments</a>. Review products of polynomials and independence of random variables.</p>',
 r'''<h3>From the joint expectation to a product</h3>'''+D(r'G_{X+Y}(t)=\mathrm E(t^{X+Y})=\mathrm E(t^Xt^Y)',r'=\mathrm E(t^X)\mathrm E(t^Y)=G_X(t)G_Y(t)\quad\text{if X,Y are independent}',r'\mathrm P(X+Y=r)=\sum_j\mathrm P(X=j)\mathrm P(Y=r-j)')+r'''<p>This coefficient sum is convolution. Include every contributing pair, weighted by its probability. Sampling without replacement can determine a hypergeometric count within one experiment; that count can still be independent of a separately randomised experiment.</p><h3>Moments from the product rule</h3>'''+D(r"G'_{X+Y}(1)=G'_X(1)+G'_Y(1)",r"G''_{X+Y}(1)=G''_X(1)+2G'_X(1)G'_Y(1)+G''_Y(1)",r'\mathrm E(X+Y)=\mathrm E(X)+\mathrm E(Y)',r'\operatorname{Var}(X+Y)=\operatorname{Var}(X)+\operatorname{Var}(Y)\quad\text{under independence}')+r'''<p>The mean identity also holds without independence when the expectations exist. The PGF product and the variance identity require independence here. Keep the product factorised for differentiation when that is shorter; expand only as far as the requested coefficient needs.</p>''',
 '<ol><li>Build each component PGF with the correct within-experiment model.</li><li>Justify independence of the component counts.</li><li>Multiply their PGFs.</li><li>Collect all contributions to the requested total.</li><li>Differentiate the product or add independently justified moments.</li></ol>',
 ['A product of PGFs requires independent counts.','Dependence between draws and dependence between final counts are different issues.','Add every coefficient contribution for a total.','A product of two coefficients of degree r does not give the degree-r coefficient of a product.','Equal means do not identify a distribution.'],[e1,e2,e3,e4])

x=(3+2*t+t**2)/6;y=t**2*x.subs(t,t**3)
e1=example('PGF-E-T1','1 · Multiply the outcome, then shift it',r'<p>\(X\) has PGF \(G_X(t)=\frac{3+2t+t^2}{6}\). For \(Y=3X+2\), find the PGF and support.</p>',
 D(r'G_Y(t)=\mathrm E(t^{3X+2})=t^2G_X(t^3)',r'=\frac{3t^2+2t^5+t^8}{6}')+'<p>The support {0,1,2} maps to {2,5,8}. Replace the input t by t³, then multiply by t². Cubing the PGF would instead describe three independent observations.</p>',{'X':x,'Y':y},dict(transform=['X','Y',3,2]))
x=(5+2*t)/7;a=x.subs(t,t**2);b=x**2
e2=example('PGF-E-T2','2 · Reusing a value is not independent repetition',r'<p>\(X\) is a binary indicator with success probability \(\frac27\). Compare \(A=2X\) with \(B=X_1+X_2\) for independent copies.</p>',
 D(r'G_A(t)=\frac{5+2t^2}{7}',r'G_B(t)=\frac{25+20t+4t^2}{49}',r'\mathrm E(A)=\mathrm E(B)=\frac47',r'\operatorname{Var}(A)=\frac{40}{49}',r'\operatorname{Var}(B)=\frac{20}{49}')+'<p>A cannot equal 1, but B can. Doubling one observation multiplies its variance by 4; adding two independent copies multiplies it by 2.</p>',{'X':x,'A':a,'B':b},dict(transform=['X','A',2,0],copies=['X','B',2]))
x=(1+t)/8+3*t**2/4;m,f,v=stats(x)
e3=example('PGF-E-T3','3 · Exclusive modes give a weighted sum',r'<p>A device uses mode A with probability \(\frac14\), otherwise mode B. Mode A outputs a fair binary count; mode B always outputs \(2\). Find the overall PGF, mean and variance.</p>',
 D(r'G_X(t)=\frac14\frac{1+t}{2}+\frac34t^2',r'=\frac{1+t+6t^2}{8}',r"G'_X(1)=\frac{13}{8},\qquad G''_X(1)=\frac32",r'\operatorname{Var}(X)=\frac32+\frac{13}{8}-\left(\frac{13}{8}\right)^2=\frac{31}{64}')+'<p>Only one mode is used. A product would describe a sum of two independently produced outputs, which is a different experiment.</p>',{'X':x},dict(mixture=['1/4','(1+t)/2','t**2']))
x=((3+2*t)/5)**3;y=s.cancel((x-x.subs(t,0))/(1-x.subs(t,0)));m,f,v=stats(y)
e4=example('PGF-E-T4','4 · Remove zero and renormalise',r'<p>\(X\) counts successes in three independent trials with probability \(\frac25\). A recorded run has at least one success; \(Y\) is the count conditional on \(X>0\). Find its PGF and mean.</p>',
 D(r'G_X(t)=\left(\frac{3+2t}{5}\right)^3,\qquad p_0=\frac{27}{125}',r'G_Y(t)=\frac{G_X(t)-p_0}{1-p_0}',r'=\frac{27t+18t^2+4t^3}{49}',r"\mathrm E(Y)=G'_Y(1)=\frac{75}{49}")+'<p>Remove just the zero coefficient, then divide every remaining coefficient by the probability of a positive run. Subtracting zero without renormalising would leave total probability below 1.</p>',{'X':x,'Y':y},dict(conditionPositive=['X','Y']))
lesson('transform','Transformations and dependence','Distinguish changing one outcome from combining independent outcomes.',
 'Transform supported values correctly and recognise products, mixtures and conditional models.',
 '<p>Complete <a href="'+base+'sums">independent sums</a>. Review conditional probability and the support of a transformed variable.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Start from the defining expectation</h3>'''+D(r'Y=aX+b\quad\Longrightarrow\quad G_Y(t)=t^bG_X(t^a)')+r'''<p>For the ordinary PGF convention, ensure the transformed values remain non-negative integers. A finite distribution can also be represented by a Laurent expression when negative outcomes occur; state the convention rather than assuming an ordinary probability power series. Always inspect the mapped support.</p><h3>One observation versus independent copies</h3>'''+D(r'G_{aX}(t)=G_X(t^a)',r'G_{X_1+\cdots+X_a}(t)=[G_X(t)]^a\quad\text{for independent identical copies}',r'\operatorname{Var}(aX)=a^2\operatorname{Var}(X)')+r'''<p>The coefficient support and variances generally differ even when the means agree. Two counts derived from the same selection can be dependent; use their joint outcomes to build a total instead of multiplying marginal PGFs.</p><h3>Mixtures and conditioning</h3>'''+D(r'G_X(t)=wG_{X\mid A}(t)+(1-w)G_{X\mid B}(t)',r'G_{X\mid X>0}(t)=\frac{G_X(t)-p_0}{1-p_0},\quad p_0=\mathrm P(X=0)<1')+r'''<p>A mixture chooses exactly one mode. Conditioning keeps a specified event and renormalises its probabilities. Neither operation is an independent-sum product.</p>''',
 '<ol><li>Identify whether the experiment transforms, sums, selects a mode or conditions.</li><li>Write the defining expectation or joint outcomes.</li><li>Use the appropriate substitution, product or weighted sum.</li><li>Check the support and normalisation.</li><li>Extract the required probabilities and moments.</li></ol>',
 ['G(t²) and G(t)² describe different experiments.','Shared observations do not become independent because their marginals are known.','A mixture uses a weighted sum of conditional PGFs.','Conditioning requires division by the retained probability.','Check the transformed support before calling an expression an ordinary PGF.'],[e1,e2,e3,e4])

x=(1+2*t+t**2)/4;z=x**3
e1=example('PGF-E-C1','1 · Three independent counts',r'<p>\(X\) has PGF \(G_X(t)=\frac{1+2t+t^2}{4}\). Let \(S\) be the sum of three independent copies. Find its PGF, \(\mathrm P(S=3)\), mean and variance.</p>',
 D(r'G_S(t)=\frac{(1+t)^6}{64}',r'\mathrm P(S=3)=\frac{\binom63}{64}=\frac5{16}',r'\mathrm E(S)=3,\qquad\operatorname{Var}(S)=\frac32')+'<p>The PGF identifies a binomial count with six fair trials. Differentiating the powered PGF gives the same moments as adding the three independent means and variances.</p>',{'X':x,'S':z},dict(copies=['X','S',3]))
x=(4*t+t**3)/5;z=x*x
e2=example('PGF-E-C2','2 · Repetition preserves gaps in possible totals',r'<p>\(X\) takes values \(1,3\) with probabilities \(\frac45,\frac15\). Two independent copies are added. Find their total’s PGF, support and probability of \(4\).</p>',
 D(r'G_S(t)=\left(\frac{4t+t^3}{5}\right)^2',r'=\frac{16t^2+8t^4+t^6}{25}',r'\mathrm P(S=4)=\frac8{25}')+'<p>The support is {2,4,6}. Total 4 comes from the two ordered outcomes (1,3) and (3,1). Odd totals remain impossible.</p>',{'X':x,'S':z},dict(copies=['X','S',2]))
x=t/(4-3*t);y=x**3
e3=example('PGF-E-C3','3 · Recover one summand',r'<p>A total of three independent identical non-negative integer-valued counts has PGF \(\frac{t^3}{(4-3t)^3}\). Recover the summand PGF and its mean.</p>',
 D(r'[G_X(t)]^3=\frac{t^3}{(4-3t)^3}',r'G_X(t)=\frac{t}{4-3t}',r"G'_X(t)=\frac{4}{(4-3t)^2},\qquad \mathrm E(X)=4")+'<p>The recovered function is a valid positive-integer waiting-time PGF. The probability-series conditions and value 1 at t=1 select the admissible branch; an arbitrary algebraic root is insufficient.</p>',{'X':x,'S':y},dict(copies=['X','S',3]))
x=(1+t)/2;z=x**4
e4=example('PGF-E-C4','4 · Condition on a total',r'<p>Four independent fair binary observations have total \(S\). Given \(S=2\), find the probability that the first observation is zero.</p>',
 D(r'\mathrm P(S=2)=[t^2]\left(\frac{1+t}{2}\right)^4=\frac6{16}',r'\mathrm P(X_1=0,S=2)=\frac12[t^2]\left(\frac{1+t}{2}\right)^3=\frac3{16}',r'\mathrm P(X_1=0\mid S=2)=\frac{3/16}{6/16}=\frac12')+'<p>Use the unconditional independent model to compute the joint event, then divide by the probability of the conditioning total. The observations are no longer independent after conditioning on that total.</p>',{'X':x,'S':z},dict(copies=['X','S',4],conditional=[4,1,2]))
lesson('copies','Repeated independent sums','Power the PGF for copies, and use coefficients to handle constrained totals.',
 'Analyse repeated independent counts, reconstruct summands and calculate conditional totals.',
 '<p>Complete <a href="'+base+'transform">transformations and dependence</a>. Review powers, coefficient extraction and conditional probability.</p>',
 r'''<h3>More than two independent variables</h3>'''+D(r'G_{X_1+\cdots+X_n}(t)=\prod_{j=1}^nG_{X_j}(t)',r'G_{S_n}(t)=[G_X(t)]^n\quad\text{for independent identical copies}')+r'''<p>Identical distributions do not by themselves give independence. With independent copies and finite moments, differentiating the power at 1 gives</p>'''+D(r"G'_{S_n}(1)=nG'_X(1)",r"G''_{S_n}(1)=nG''_X(1)+n(n-1)[G'_X(1)]^2",r'\mathrm E(S_n)=n\mathrm E(X)',r'\operatorname{Var}(S_n)=n\operatorname{Var}(X)')+r'''<h3>Keep coefficient calculations local</h3><p>When an external power of t shifts the support, remove that shift before choosing a binomial term. Several ordered combinations can give the same total. A conditional event requires its joint coefficient divided by the total-event coefficient.</p><h3>Recovering the summand</h3><p>If a total is known to be a sum of independent identical variables, its PGF is a power of the unknown summand PGF. Use coefficient constraints and non-negativity, or a recognised factorisation, to find the admissible root. Check the result by raising it back to the required power.</p>''',
 '<ol><li>Check independence and whether all copies have the same distribution.</li><li>Form the product or power.</li><li>Track the minimum total and any support gaps.</li><li>Extract coefficients or differentiate before evaluating.</li><li>For a conditional question divide the joint-event coefficient by the conditioning-event coefficient.</li></ol>',
 ['Identical distribution is not independence.','Do not replace a power of G(t) by G(tⁿ).','Adjust the requested degree for an external power of t.','Keep every ordered contribution to a coefficient.','A reconstructed root must have non-negative coefficients and normalise to one.'],[e1,e2,e3,e4])
